   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Let \\( g(x) = \\int_{x^{3}}^{2} \\sqrt{t + 1} \\, dt \\). Find the exact value of \\( g'\\left(2\\right) \\)."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": -36
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Reversing the limits, \\( g(x) = -\\int_{2}^{x^{3}} \\sqrt{t + 1} \\, dt \\), so by the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus its derivative is minus the integrand evaluated at \\( x^{3} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Negate",
            [
               "Sqrt",
               [
                  "Add",
                  [
                     "Power",
                     "x",
                     3
                  ],
                  1
               ]
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The chain rule multiplies by the derivative of \\( x^{3} \\), which is \\( 3 x^{2} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Multiply",
            3,
            [
               "Power",
               "x",
               2
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "At \\( x = 2 \\) the limit is \\( 8 \\) and its derivative is \\( 12 \\).",
         "mathjson": 12
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "Using \\( f\\left(8\\right) = 3 \\), \\( g'\\left(2\\right) = -3 \\cdot 12 = -36 \\).",
         "mathjson": -36
      }
   ],
